current directory, which

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:05.380
means our current module, send
that up to Google servers.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.940
It will build that code,
make sure it's working,

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:13.120
and then make that endpoint
URL available, ideally.

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.460
And so in this case,
I'm using HTTP,

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.130
but there's one other
type of Cloud Function

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:19.660
that you can write.

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.910
Those are background functions.

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:25.990
And background functions
respond to events that happen.

00:06:25.990 --> 00:06:29.390
And in a way, you can
think of visiting some HTTP

00:06:29.390 --> 00:06:34.930
URL as an event that happens,
but what I mean in this case is

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.550
something like Pub/Sub.

00:06:36.550 --> 00:06:39.820
So Pub/Sub is this
distributed message bus

00:06:39.820 --> 00:06:44.740
where you can send messages into
it on a topic that you define,

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:47.270
and then subscribe
to it other places.

00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.780
And if you're running on, say,
Compute Engine or App Engine

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:52.750
or somewhere else, you
might subscribe manually

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:54.520
to that topic.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.770
With Cloud Functions, you
don't have to do that.

00:06:57.770 --> 00:07:01.240
You just say, attach this Cloud
Function to this Pub/Sub topic.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.160
Every message that
gets published here,

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:04.680
trigger this Cloud Function.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.670
So that's really nice.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:12.100
In this case, we have
an HTTPS function.

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:15.550
You can visit it, click that
link, and you get Hello, World.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:18.130
Awesome.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:25.430
And so you can also do
things like Cloud storage

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:28.850
where, let's say, a user
uploads a profile picture

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:30.710
and you need to
process that picture

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:33.440
to, say, make a
thumbnail that you can

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:34.920
display on your application.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.760
That's the perfect use
case for Cloud Functions

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:40.700
because you send that into
a Cloud Storage bucket,

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:42.650
your function gets
triggered, processed,

00:07:42.650 --> 00:07:45.170
do whatever image
manipulation you need to do,

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:48.200
and start somewhere else.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:52.250
But that doesn't always
make the most sense

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.200
for your entire application.

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:57.470
You might have something that's,
say, directly user facing.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:00.770
Maybe it has multiple endpoints
that are all working together

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:03.710
and the user travels
between them.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:05.750
In that case, it
might make more sense

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.220
to use something
like App Engine.

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:12.420
And with App Engine, you
get an entire HTTP binary

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.310
that you can be running.

00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:19.270
So again, let's kind of take a
look at what that looks like.

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:23.850
So we have our go.mod
file, our hello.go file,

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:27.970
and we're going to create one
more directory called cmd.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:32.990
And in cmd, we are going
to create a main package--

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:34.429
