In this case, I'll
stick with 111 SGA.

00:13:36.203 --> 00:13:37.620
And then we need
another line that

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.900
says what is the main package?

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.820
What is the binary that we
are going to be running?

00:13:42.820 --> 00:13:45.180
In that case, or
in this case, it's

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:47.230
in the command sub directory.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:50.040
And this is helpful for modules
because as you're developing

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:51.600
your application,
your binary might

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:53.910
be a few directories
deep, and that's

00:13:53.910 --> 00:13:55.830
super common and super
helpful and a nice way

00:13:55.830 --> 00:13:57.850
to design your application.

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:02.100
But for App Engine, you need
to deploy that entire module

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.800
so you can import
everything that's needed.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:07.470
We have our binary
importing hello, so we need

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:10.380
this entire module directory.

00:14:10.380 --> 00:14:15.390
So we say the main command
is cmd, save, and then

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:17.910
we can run G Cloud app.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.470
Deploy.

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:22.590
And this is going to go through
the same kind of build process

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:25.200
that we went through for
Cloud Functions where

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:30.760
the current code gets zipped
up, sent to Cloud Storage--

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:33.390
you can see uploading three
files to Cloud Storage--

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:36.270
it builds on Google
servers, makes

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:38.250
sure that the application
works, and then

00:14:38.250 --> 00:14:40.570
deploys it at your end point.

00:14:40.570 --> 00:14:42.623
So you might have
some custom domain,

00:14:42.623 --> 00:14:44.040
or you can see
this is going to go

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:46.350
at
serverless-gopher.appspot.com.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:56.340
So this is using App Engine
standard and, specifically,

00:14:56.340 --> 00:15:00.420
App Engine standard second
gen, or second generation.

00:15:00.420 --> 00:15:04.350
And that's built on top of
gVisor, which gives Sandbox

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:07.300
security to your application.

00:15:07.300 --> 00:15:12.870
So that means any package
that you want to use, you can.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:15.660
In first generation run
times on App Engine,

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:17.670
you had to use specific
packages to do things

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.950
like HTTP requests.

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:24.180
So you would go to use
just standard HTTP default

00:15:24.180 --> 00:15:27.300
client get, and then you
would get some runtime failure

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:28.800
when you deployed
your application

00:15:28.800 --> 00:15:30.870
saying you can't do that.

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:32.470
Same thing with contexts.

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:34.530
Now you can use whatever
context you want.

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:38.470
You can just grab the
request context directly,

00:15:38.470 --> 00:15:40.350
and you don't have
to worry about it.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:43.860
App Engine flexible is
similar to App Engine standard

00:15:43.860 --> 00:15:47.220
except you get to--

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:49.890
one, it has a minimum
instance count of one.

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:51.810
So with standard, you
can go down to zero.

00:15:51.810 --> 00:15:54.780
Flex, you have to
have a minimum of one.

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.660
That means you're being
charged as it runs.

00:15:57.660 --> 00:16:00.000
If no one's using it, you're
still paying money for it.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.560
Not very nice to use.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:03.810
A nice thing is that you
get container images,

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:07.910
but I'll talk about container
images after I show you this.

00:16:07.910 --> 00:16:11.320
So let's take this URL.

00:16:11.320 --> 00:16:12.800
We just deployed it.

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:14.320
Copy.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:17.780
We defined the /hello end
point, and we get Hello, World.

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:18.403
Awesome.

00:16:18.403 --> 00:16:20.320
I'll call out here, we
didn't make any changes

00:16:20.320 --> 00:16:22.472
to the Cloud Function
that we deployed,

00:16:22.472 --> 00:16:23.680
and that's kind of the point.

00:16:23.680 --> 00:16:25.870
The code that you're
writing shouldn't

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:29.260
be dependent on where
you're deploying it.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:32.060
Kind of the Go code in one
place is the Go code in another.

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:37.400
But sometimes you
need one more level

00:16:37.400 --> 00:16:42.830
of granularity, higher than,
say, App Engine, or just

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:44.520
the binary.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.870
And so that might be because
you need additional tools that

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:50.270
are right next to your server
that your server might,

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:51.980
say, shell out to.

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:56.270
Or you define some
native code that

00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:59.090
depends on the type of
machine that's running it.

00:16:59.090 --> 00:17:03.380
And in those cases, you
can use a container.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:06.200
And containers can be
deployed in a serverless way--

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:09.260
same kind of scaling up
and down as you need--

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:10.819
with Cloud Run.

00:17:10.819 --> 00:17:13.040
So Cloud Run, announced,
again, a couple of days

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:17.480
ago, does serverless containers.

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:20.670
So you say, I'm going to
deploy this container,

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:24.650
or I'm pushing it and I'm
storing it on Google Container

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:30.210
Registry, and then deploy
that and it scales as needed.

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:34.190
So let's take a look at
what that looks like.

00:17:34.190 --> 00:17:36.440
We're going to need to
define our container

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:40.610
in some way, and a super
common way to do that--

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:42.950
most common-- is
with a Docker file.

00:17:42.950 --> 00:17:46.420
So I have one already written.

00:17:46.420 --> 00:17:47.857
Ignore the fact that says buggy.

00:17:47.857 --> 00:17:48.940
I'll get to that in a sec.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:54.430
And let's take a look
at this Docker file.

00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:58.810
So we start on line one here and
we say from golang as builder,

00:17:58.810 --> 00:18:01.780
this is the kind of root image
that we're going to be building

00:18:01.780 --> 00:18:08.020
our container image in, say,
work in the /home directory.

00:18:08.020 --> 00:18:10.750
And I'll call out for Go
applications, this line

00:18:10.750 --> 00:18:13.460
five and six is really nice.

00:18:13.460 --> 00:18:16.780
This says copy in the
go.mod and go.sum files

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:19.150
and then run go.mod download.

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:20.650
And that's helpful
because these are

00:18:20.650 --> 00:18:24.380
run in different Docker stages,
and they get cached as needed.

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:27.290
So if your go.mod and your
go.sum files don't change,

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:30.100
then you don't have to
rerun go.mod download.

00:18:30.100 --> 00:18:33.250
That's really nice when you're
developing because you aren't

00:18:33.250 --> 00:18:36.910
changing your dependencies
every time you save and test

00:18:36.910 --> 00:18:40.900
or deploy, so you
shouldn't have to download

00:18:40.900 --> 00:18:43.180
all of those dependencies
every single time

00:18:43.180 --> 00:18:45.042
you try to build your container.

00:18:45.042 --> 00:18:47.500
So that means it might take a
couple minutes the first time

00:18:47.500 --> 00:18:49.930
because you have to
download everything, right,

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.060
and then you make some
modification to your code,

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:55.360
and then it's just basically
the compile time plus whatever

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:56.920
it takes for Docker to start.

00:18:56.920 --> 00:18:57.555
Really nice.

00:18:57.555 --> 00:18:58.180
I recommend it.

00:18:58.180 --> 00:19:00.910
Regardless of where you're
building and running your code,

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:03.790
do this if you're
running some container.

00:19:03.790 --> 00:19:06.660
Line seven says copy
the rest of it in.

00:19:06.660 --> 00:19:08.730
We compile our binary.

00:19:08.730 --> 00:19:10.410
And then this is
done as a multi-stage

00:19:10.410 --> 00:19:14.820
build where the second
stage is done from Alpine.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:18.210
And Alpine is a trimmed
down, smaller base image

00:19:18.210 --> 00:19:21.060
that doesn't have extra things
that aren't really needed

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:24.660
to run a static binary,
which you get with Go.

00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:28.800
But one thing we do
need is certificates.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:32.400
And certificates are
useful for, basically,

00:19:32.400 --> 00:19:35.490
if you're doing anything
with SSL or HTTPS,

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:38.130
you'll want to
include this line.

00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:39.450
Finally, we copy our binary.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:40.890
Make sure it's executable.

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:42.480
Set that port
environment variable

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.840
that I defined or
talked about earlier.

00:19:45.840 --> 00:19:48.090
And then the command
to start it is /hello.

00:19:50.840 --> 00:19:59.080
So I deployed this already
on Google Container Registry,

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:03.800
and you can see that at
gcr.io/serverless-gopher.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:07.000
And this is the
project that I created.

00:20:07.000 --> 00:20:09.260
And you can see I have
one image in here.

00:20:09.260 --> 00:20:12.600
It's called helloworld.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.970
And there are two
versions of this.

00:20:14.970 --> 00:20:21.180
One is 1.0.0, and the
other is 1.0.0-buggy.

00:20:21.180 --> 00:20:23.440
So like I said, I'll get
back to buggy in a second,

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:27.720
but for right now, I'm going
to apply the 1.0 version.

00:20:27.720 --> 00:20:31.950
And to do that, we are
going to use G Cloud again.

00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:36.010
So G Cloud beta.

00:20:36.010 --> 00:20:36.610
Run.

00:20:36.610 --> 00:20:37.600
Deploy.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:40.720
And then the one argument
we need is image.

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:45.370
And this is going to be
exactly what that image I just

00:20:45.370 --> 00:20:46.380
showed you was.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:55.620
So gcr.io/serverless
gopher/helloworld and then you

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:57.810
can also specify your version.

00:20:57.810 --> 00:20:59.430
The default version is latest.

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:01.530
In this case, I'm
going to do 1.0.0

00:21:01.530 --> 00:21:02.890
which we just talked about.

00:21:02.890 --> 00:21:05.490
So we hit Deploy here,
and this time, we

00:21:05.490 --> 00:21:08.725
aren't copying the source
from the current directory.

00:21:08.725 --> 00:21:11.100
It's just going to download
that image from the container

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:12.440
registry.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:16.110
I'm going specify US central.

00:21:16.110 --> 00:21:19.500
Service name-- it defaults
to the image name,

00:21:19.500 --> 00:21:22.820
and we get this deploying
container image.

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:24.840
We get one option here--

00:21:24.840 --> 00:21:28.020
allow unauthenticated
invocations to service.

00:21:28.020 --> 00:21:29.490
Yes.

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:32.160
That basically means we don't
need to log in to hit this,

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:34.920
and it's just a little
bit more convenient,

00:21:34.920 --> 00:21:38.250
at least for this demo.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:43.140
And so here, it creates a new
service, pulls down that image,

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:45.750
starts it up, performs
a health check,

00:21:45.750 --> 00:21:48.390
reroutes traffic to your
new version as needed,

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:53.070
and as you can see, it
literally took a few seconds.

00:21:53.070 --> 00:21:56.170
Let's open this URL just
to make sure it works.

00:21:56.170 --> 00:22:00.000
We're at /hello and
we get Hello, World.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:01.000
Very cool.

00:22:01.000 --> 00:22:05.710
And so kind of from so far--

00:22:05.710 --> 00:22:08.867
we started with HTTP functions.

00:22:08.867 --> 00:22:10.950
You might have many of
these for your application.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:12.660
They might do many
different things.

00:22:12.660 --> 00:22:14.327
You might have some
background functions

00:22:14.327 --> 00:22:17.400
that are part of your app.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.640
But either way, you start
with these HTTP handlers.

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:23.790
Maybe you say, I need a
little bit more control.

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:27.060
I want to bundle all
of this up together.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:30.540
I want to not have these
independently scalable small,

00:22:30.540 --> 00:22:33.540
stateless bits of code
called Cloud Functions.

00:22:33.540 --> 00:22:36.720
I'm going to deploy this
as a single binary HTTP

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:40.320
server that's running.

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:42.840
And then if that's not
quite enough, or you need,

00:22:42.840 --> 00:22:45.750
like I said those native
binaries, or those extra tools

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:49.380
right next to it, you might add
a container image around that

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:51.820
and deploy that on Cloud Run.

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:55.650
So, for Go specific, I've
found it really helpful

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:59.460
to have these containers
to build a Go funct

00:22:59.460 --> 00:23:01.770
as a service with a co-worker.

00:23:01.770 --> 00:23:04.710
So that you could just
bundle in those Go tools

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:07.320
that you needed
in your container.

00:23:07.320 --> 00:23:10.940
When you're running on App
Engine, is Go installed?

00:23:10.940 --> 00:23:12.070
I don't know.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:13.970
If you run on a
container, I do know

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:17.180
because you say in that
Docker file, yes, I need Go,

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:19.730
or I need ImageMagick.

00:23:19.730 --> 00:23:24.760
ImageMagick is included in App
Engine, but you get the point.

00:23:24.760 --> 00:23:28.680
So that's where I've
really liked using it.

00:23:28.680 --> 00:23:32.280
But kind of looking at the
big picture here, running it

00:23:32.280 --> 00:23:33.755
isn't the end of the story.

00:23:33.755 --> 00:23:35.130
The point of
running something is

00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:36.860
to do something awesome with it.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:38.693
And you might have all
of these other things

00:23:38.693 --> 00:23:40.630
around it, like you're
using ML, Cloud Vision,

00:23:40.630 --> 00:23:44.860
or building some
chat bot, and so on.

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:47.850
And the point is not just
to have that code running

00:23:47.850 --> 00:23:48.510
on App Engine.

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:50.000
It's to do something
cool with it.

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:58.040
And regardless, you kind of
have to maintain that and keep

00:23:58.040 --> 00:23:58.797
it going.

00:23:58.797 --> 00:24:00.380
So now that we have
something running,

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:03.500
let's talk and shift gears into
talking about maintaining it

00:24:03.500 --> 00:24:05.600
and keeping it going.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:09.330
In the first line of defense
that I really see is testing.

00:24:09.330 --> 00:24:11.300
So it's not really a fun topic.

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:14.680
It's kind of a boring
topic maybe sometimes,

00:24:14.680 --> 00:24:16.250
but what I want to
call out here is

00:24:16.250 --> 00:24:21.710
that testing for deploying on
GCF or App Engine or Cloud Run

00:24:21.710 --> 00:24:23.180
is just testing.

00:24:23.180 --> 00:24:24.980
You aren't running
something special that

00:24:24.980 --> 00:24:29.330
says because I am running
on Cloud Functions,

00:24:29.330 --> 00:24:31.480
I need this extra stuff.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:33.102
It should just be
a normal Go test.

00:24:33.102 --> 00:24:34.810
If you've ever written
Go code, if you've

00:24:34.810 --> 00:24:38.440
written Go test before,
this is what they look like.

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:40.660
In this case, this is
using the HTTP test

00:24:40.660 --> 00:24:42.310
package from the
standard library

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:46.750
and checks the response
code as data's OK.

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:50.350
If you do have kind of multiple
things going on and you want

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:52.540
to test that altogether--
you have production--

00:24:52.540 --> 00:24:55.450
I recommend creating
test projects as needed.

00:24:55.450 --> 00:24:58.090
And your test project can
be completely independent

00:24:58.090 --> 00:24:59.998
of all of your
production resources,

00:24:59.998 --> 00:25:01.540
and then you don't
have to have worry

00:25:01.540 --> 00:25:05.380
about running out or
accidentally deleting something

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:09.567
from production if it's just
a completely separate Google

00:25:09.567 --> 00:25:10.150
Cloud project.

00:25:13.425 --> 00:25:15.300
The other thing to keep
in mind-- and this is

00:25:15.300 --> 00:25:17.550
kind of true for any
microservice application

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:21.070
that you're building--
is implicit dependencies.

00:25:21.070 --> 00:25:24.570
So I built one
application where users

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:26.670
could go to a
website and request

00:25:26.670 --> 00:25:29.280
translations of certain text.

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:32.310
So you get free form input
for what text, and then

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:35.040
a selector box for what
language should that text

00:25:35.040 --> 00:25:36.930
be translated to.

00:25:36.930 --> 00:25:38.640
And so that request--

00:25:38.640 --> 00:25:44.110
that form post response--
gets sent to some server.

00:25:44.110 --> 00:25:46.950
And then I didn't want
to run that translation

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:49.098
right there, right
then because it

00:25:49.098 --> 00:25:50.390
might take a little bit longer.

00:25:50.390 --> 00:25:51.750
It might take 100 milliseconds.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:53.760
It might take more.

00:25:53.760 --> 00:25:56.790
So what I did was I published
all of those to Pub/Sub,

00:25:56.790 --> 00:26:01.290
and I said here on this
topic, go translate this text

00:26:01.290 --> 00:26:03.210
into French.

00:26:03.210 --> 00:26:04.950
And then I created
a Cloud Function

00:26:04.950 --> 00:26:08.220
that listened on that Pub/Sub
topic, brought things in,

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:11.520
said OK, I'm going to take
that text, translate to French,

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:14.160
store it in the database,
and then the user refreshes

00:26:14.160 --> 00:26:16.290
and they get the
list of translations

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:17.550
from that database.

00:26:17.550 --> 00:26:18.983
That's awesome.

00:26:18.983 --> 00:26:21.150
But you kind of have this
implicit dependency there.

00:26:21.150 --> 00:26:23.570
You have one code that's
pushing into Pub/Sub

00:26:23.570 --> 00:26:27.320
and this other code that's
pulling down from Pub/Sub,

00:26:27.320 --> 00:26:29.450
and they have that implicit
dependency that says,

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:32.850
what is the format of that data
that's going back and forth?

00:26:32.850 --> 00:26:35.240
If you're calling it
directly, you're writing Go,

00:26:35.240 --> 00:26:37.820
it just type checks,
it compiles--

00:26:37.820 --> 00:26:38.930
you're good.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:40.430
But if you're going
through Pub/Sub,

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:43.220
if you're publishing
events and so on,

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:45.720
you have to be a
lot more careful.

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:48.030
So I don't have a hard
and fast rule that says,

00:26:48.030 --> 00:26:49.850
what's the best
way to manage this?

00:26:49.850 --> 00:26:53.210
But documenting it, having
a good clear picture

00:26:53.210 --> 00:26:57.180
of where all of the data of
your application is going,

00:26:57.180 --> 00:26:58.130
will be super helpful.

00:27:00.950 --> 00:27:03.240
And the other issue
is visibility.

00:27:03.240 --> 00:27:05.030
So it's like, yeah,
sure, Tyler, you're

00:27:05.030 --> 00:27:07.170
running all these containers
that go up and down.

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:11.270
You have, whatever,
25 containers here.

00:27:11.270 --> 00:27:13.460
What's inside these containers?

00:27:13.460 --> 00:27:15.140
30.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:16.230
It's OK.

00:27:16.230 --> 00:27:18.270
What's inside these containers?

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:20.900
Anyone?

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:22.340
I don't know.

00:27:22.340 --> 00:27:25.010
So you need that extra
visibility to say,

00:27:25.010 --> 00:27:26.330
what's inside these?

00:27:26.330 --> 00:27:28.730
How are they communicating
back and forth?

00:27:28.730 --> 00:27:30.230
Is it fast?

00:27:30.230 --> 00:27:31.980
Can I debug it?

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:36.090
And the way to do that, or at
least one way, is with tracing.

00:27:36.090 --> 00:27:40.010
So tracing-- there's a package
or a library called OpenCensus,

00:27:40.010 --> 00:27:42.980
and this is instrumented
in multiple languages--

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:44.660
in this case, I'm show Go--

00:27:44.660 --> 00:27:47.180
but the same principles apply.

00:27:47.180 --> 00:27:50.450
You start by creating
spans and then

00:27:50.450 --> 00:27:53.760
you end those spans at
some point in the future.

00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:55.970
In this case, I'm
creating a span called

00:27:55.970 --> 00:27:59.480
example.com/hello.hello.

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:02.210
And the convention for creating
these is you take your package

00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:04.970
name dot the function name.

00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:08.360
That way, later, if you
ever need to debug and find

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:10.640
the code that's
creating this span,

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:12.660
you know exactly where to go.

00:28:12.660 --> 00:28:15.930
And then this call
is defer span.end.

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:18.170
And defer means
this end call will

00:28:18.170 --> 00:28:23.070
run when the function returns,
or at the end of the function.

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:25.670
And then what you get from
this is it returns a context,

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.470
and as you pass
that context around

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:29.240
through your
application, you get

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:33.330
to see these other
spans that are created.

00:28:33.330 --> 00:28:35.810
And as you create a new
span from this context,

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:40.400
it all gets attributed to the
parent, or top level span,

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:42.380
that created this.

00:28:42.380 --> 00:28:44.150
So we'll see an
example of what this

00:28:44.150 --> 00:28:47.510
looks like in a little bit.

00:28:47.510 --> 00:28:50.570
Once you do that, you have to
export that data somewhere.

00:28:50.570 --> 00:28:55.730
So like I said, OpenCensus is
open source multiple languages.

00:28:55.730 --> 00:28:58.310
One benefit of that is
that the exporter can

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:00.450
be whatever you want it to be.

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:03.170
So that might be
Stackdriver, which

00:29:03.170 --> 00:29:04.460
I have an example of here.

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:07.760
Stackdriver is Google Cloud's
application performance

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:10.040
monitoring suite.

00:29:10.040 --> 00:29:12.740
But it might be
Datadog, or it might

00:29:12.740 --> 00:29:19.000
be some local just print it to
standard out as I'm developing.

00:29:19.000 --> 00:29:21.760
And that makes it
really easy to recommend

00:29:21.760 --> 00:29:24.170
tracing throughout
your entire code

00:29:24.170 --> 00:29:25.840
because if you
ever want to change

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:30.160
where you're exporting that
data later, it doesn't matter.

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:32.830
It's a small, maybe 10
line, configuration change

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:34.770
that says export
this somewhere else.

00:29:38.890 --> 00:29:40.800
The other side of
kind of the same coin

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:43.590
is tracing you have
to explicitly add--

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:45.940
or maybe there are
plugins to help you.

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:49.440
For example, there is a
OpenCensus HTTP plugin.

00:29:49.440 --> 00:29:51.870
So you just say trace
all of these handlers,

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:53.370
and it just works.

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:55.200
The other side of that
coin, without having

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:58.320
to do much manual
work, is profiling.

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.690
And profiling
gives you, say, CPU

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:06.240
or heap usage of your code
by calling this start call.

00:30:06.240 --> 00:30:07.770
So what's this look like?

00:30:07.770 --> 00:30:09.420
Again, let's do a demo.

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:15.790
You may have guessed
already, but let's

00:30:15.790 --> 00:30:19.610
deploy that buggy version
that I talked about earlier.

00:30:19.610 --> 00:30:22.460
So we look here.

00:30:22.460 --> 00:30:26.150
We have 1.0 and 1.0.0-buggy.

00:30:26.150 --> 00:30:29.280
Let's deploy this on Cloud Run.

00:30:29.280 --> 00:30:35.010
And, for this version,
I made a few changes.

00:30:35.010 --> 00:30:38.130
One or two of them
are I added tracing

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:40.660
and I added profiling,
which I just talked about,

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:43.440
and then I also
added two other bugs.

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:47.370
So my hope is that I can deploy
this, throw some traffic at it

00:30:47.370 --> 00:30:49.177
just to make sure those
bugs get triggered,

00:30:49.177 --> 00:30:50.760
and then we can go
look at the tracing

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:53.280
and profiling tools
in the Cloud console

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:56.580
to hopefully find those bugs.

00:30:56.580 --> 00:30:59.670
So we'll stick with us-central1.

00:30:59.670 --> 00:31:01.300
Same service name.

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:02.370
This will deploy.

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:06.630
It should take just a
couple of seconds here.

00:31:10.070 --> 00:31:12.770
Doing the same thing.

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:17.500
Assigning traffic to
the latest revision.

00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:18.670
And done.

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:24.650
I'll use curl just to make
sure this is still working.

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:27.940
Oops.

00:31:27.940 --> 00:31:30.030
/hello.

00:31:30.030 --> 00:31:32.820
Go delete all of
this extra stuff.

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:38.700
Cool.

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:40.140
We're still getting
Hello, World.

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:41.640
Everything is still working.

00:31:41.640 --> 00:31:45.930
No obvious bugs, at least
from this perspective.

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:51.010
That one took a
little bit longer.

00:31:51.010 --> 00:31:54.400
So one option-- we could send
a few thousand curl requests

00:31:54.400 --> 00:31:57.400
and we could go home
sometime around dinner.

00:31:57.400 --> 00:32:00.470
Or there's another
tool called hey.

00:32:00.470 --> 00:32:06.280
And what hey can do
is we can take this--

00:32:06.280 --> 00:32:15.700
clear hey-- and -z is going
to be how long we run for.

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:18.480
All right.

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:21.220
And hey was bought by a
coworker of mine, JBD,

00:32:21.220 --> 00:32:22.670
and it's written
in Go, and it can

00:32:22.670 --> 00:32:27.280
send a configurable amount
of traffic at the URL

00:32:27.280 --> 00:32:28.330
that you specify.

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:31.360
So in this case, we're going
to say run for two minutes,

00:32:31.360 --> 00:32:34.790
send a bunch of requests
to this end point.

00:32:34.790 --> 00:32:39.410
While that's going, over
here, let's open trace.

00:32:42.250 --> 00:32:44.620
And I'll call out, on
this overview page,

00:32:44.620 --> 00:32:49.510
there are reports of the
different traces that you get.

00:32:49.510 --> 00:32:54.040
So you might get a distribution
of how long that trace took.

00:32:54.040 --> 00:32:56.920
You can see latency and
request percentiles,

00:32:56.920 --> 00:33:01.080
and then example traces that
correspond to those requests.

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:05.710
In this case, let's just
look at real time responses

00:33:05.710 --> 00:33:08.010
that are coming in.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:15.000
And I'll restrict this to
example.com/hello.hello which

00:33:15.000 --> 00:33:18.560
was the span that we care about.

00:33:18.560 --> 00:33:22.520
Zoom in on recent code.

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:23.960
And immediately
calling out here,

00:33:23.960 --> 00:33:26.090
we have a bunch of
requests at the bottom.

00:33:26.090 --> 00:33:27.050
Those look fine.

00:33:27.050 --> 00:33:28.370
Those look like about zero.

00:33:28.370 --> 00:33:29.820
Great.

00:33:29.820 --> 00:33:32.630
We have another line at
about 300 milliseconds.

00:33:32.630 --> 00:33:35.420
That's probably not what we're
looking for a hello world

00:33:35.420 --> 00:33:36.260
application.

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:38.450
So that's something
to look into.

00:33:38.450 --> 00:33:40.040
And then the third
group is we have

00:33:40.040 --> 00:33:44.610
all of these clustered kind of
in the middle, random latency

00:33:44.610 --> 00:33:45.950
traces.

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:52.350
And so just to say the x-axis
on this is the timeline.

00:33:52.350 --> 00:33:55.310
And so as you go more left,
you get older and older.

00:33:55.310 --> 00:34:01.100
And then the y-axis is how
long it took in milliseconds.

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:04.940
And so when we click one
of these dots down here,

00:34:04.940 --> 00:34:07.730
we get this timeline
at the bottom, which

00:34:07.730 --> 00:34:09.620
includes all of our spans.

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:12.840
So this request took
zero milliseconds.

00:34:12.840 --> 00:34:16.250
I would say that's an
acceptable amount of time.

00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:18.488
All of these are taking zero.

00:34:18.488 --> 00:34:20.030
And then you can
see we have this one

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:21.989
span of the hello span.

00:34:21.989 --> 00:34:24.630
And let's look up here
at 300 milliseconds.

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:27.350
So we have our hello
span, and then inside

00:34:27.350 --> 00:34:30.239
of that there is a slow span.

00:34:30.239 --> 00:34:33.239
And this is eating up that
entire 300 milliseconds.

00:34:33.239 --> 00:34:38.407
So looking at the name of this,
we have example.com/hello.slow.

00:34:38.407 --> 00:34:39.949
It's pretty clear
where we can look--

00:34:39.949 --> 00:34:42.560
when we take a look at the
source code that I deployed

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:43.909
in a little bit--

00:34:43.909 --> 00:34:46.100
where we can look and
hopefully find out why this

00:34:46.100 --> 00:34:48.620
is taking 300 milliseconds.

00:34:48.620 --> 00:34:50.690
All of these other
ones in here--

00:34:50.690 --> 00:34:53.480
96 milliseconds-- 95.

00:34:53.480 --> 00:34:54.739
Why?

00:34:54.739 --> 00:34:56.929
You look here, you have spans.

00:34:56.929 --> 00:34:57.777
Nothing is there.

00:34:57.777 --> 00:34:58.860
Nothing is really helpful.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:00.530
Nothing's showing up.

00:35:00.530 --> 00:35:02.510
But it's still taking
95 milliseconds.

00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:05.960
What is there to do about that?

00:35:05.960 --> 00:35:11.300
So astute gopher, as
you can probably guess,

00:35:11.300 --> 00:35:14.130
we have profiling.

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:17.190
And profiling-- the
default here is CPU time.

00:35:17.190 --> 00:35:18.865
You also want to double check--

00:35:18.865 --> 00:35:20.490
so you have hello
world and hello world

00:35:20.490 --> 00:35:22.710
test as your services--

00:35:22.710 --> 00:35:24.450
check that you're
using the right service

00:35:24.450 --> 00:35:26.220
or looking at the
right service here.

00:35:26.220 --> 00:35:28.007
I've spent more time
than I care to admit

00:35:28.007 --> 00:35:29.590
looking at the wrong
one and wondering

00:35:29.590 --> 00:35:31.280
why profiling wasn't there.

00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:34.410
Just take it from my experience.

00:35:34.410 --> 00:35:37.110
You can specify what version
you want to look for.

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:40.520
Let's say what time frame
you want to look at.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:44.420
And immediately jumping
out here is Fibonacci.

00:35:44.420 --> 00:35:46.390
And you can probably
guess what this is doing,

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:50.180
but before I get there, this
is called a flame graph.

00:35:50.180 --> 00:35:53.640
And a flame graph is
giving you some metric--

00:35:53.640 --> 00:35:55.830
in this case, it's CPU
time, but like I said,

00:35:55.830 --> 00:35:58.740
it could also be say
a heap allocation--

00:35:58.740 --> 00:36:02.430
where on the x-axis, you
have how much of that metric

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:05.880
did this function call
take across the profiles

00:36:05.880 --> 00:36:07.440
that we've received?

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:09.750
On the y-axis, you
have the stack depth

00:36:09.750 --> 00:36:11.910
of the functions
that were called.

00:36:11.910 --> 00:36:15.360
So if we start at the top here,
we have some standard library

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:18.870
things at connection.serve,
serve handler, serve HTTP,

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:21.270
and so on, until
we get to hello,

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:23.760
which was the handler
that we wrote.

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:26.640
And then right underneath
this, we're calling Fibonacci.

00:36:26.640 --> 00:36:31.230
And Fibonacci is a very
wide, very deep stack thing,

00:36:31.230 --> 00:36:36.360
and we'll see what the code
is for this in a second.

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:38.170
Not every time you
look at profiling

00:36:38.170 --> 00:36:40.350
it's going to be this
obvious, but it's just

00:36:40.350 --> 00:36:44.880
another tool in the box to check
of where is the CPU time going?

00:36:44.880 --> 00:36:48.050
Why is this request
taking 100 milliseconds?

00:36:48.050 --> 00:36:50.740
Look at profiling-- very clear.

00:36:50.740 --> 00:36:58.000
So let's take a look at
what that looks like.

00:36:58.000 --> 00:37:02.970
So let's do cd into buggy.

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:05.310
This is all the same
code that I wrote before.

00:37:05.310 --> 00:37:08.310
There's no app.yml here
because since we're just

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:11.100
deploying a container image
and not on App Engine,

00:37:11.100 --> 00:37:14.280
we don't need that app.yml so
you won't see it right here.

00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:18.030
All of the other code is the
same except for the changes

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:19.570
that I made.

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:23.120
So let's take a look
at what that looks for.

00:37:23.120 --> 00:37:24.310
We will delete that.

00:37:24.310 --> 00:37:26.320
Not worried about swap.

00:37:26.320 --> 00:37:27.070
All right.

00:37:27.070 --> 00:37:32.730
You can see we have extra
imports here of opencensus.io.

00:37:32.730 --> 00:37:36.130
And then there is the
Stackdriver exporter variable

00:37:36.130 --> 00:37:38.100
on line 15.

00:37:38.100 --> 00:37:40.650
And then in an init
function, which

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:43.440
is run during package
initialization for your Go

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:44.880
program.

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:47.070
I create a new
Stackdriver exporter,

00:37:47.070 --> 00:37:48.390
just like I showed you.

00:37:48.390 --> 00:37:51.810
Register that as
the trace exporter.

00:37:51.810 --> 00:37:54.367
And then line 24 here,
I want to call this out.

00:37:54.367 --> 00:37:55.950
As you're developing
your application,

00:37:55.950 --> 00:37:57.540
you're adding tracing,
you say, yeah,

00:37:57.540 --> 00:37:59.040
export my data to Stackdriver.

00:37:59.040 --> 00:37:59.950
You do it.

00:37:59.950 --> 00:38:02.100
Nothing shows up.

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:03.790
That's because,
by default, there

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:06.860
is sampling that goes
into all of the traces.

00:38:06.860 --> 00:38:09.140
You don't necessarily
need to sample

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:12.750
or send traces for every
single request that comes in.

00:38:12.750 --> 00:38:17.472
So you can sample, say,
1% or 0.5%, or 0.1%,

00:38:17.472 --> 00:38:19.430
depending on the level
of traffic that you need

00:38:19.430 --> 00:38:22.430
and the visibility into all
of the requests that you have.

00:38:22.430 --> 00:38:24.830
When you're debugging, you
want maximum visibility.

00:38:24.830 --> 00:38:26.450
You're not concerned about QPS.

00:38:26.450 --> 00:38:27.950
Just show me the traces.

00:38:27.950 --> 00:38:28.858
Are they working?

00:38:28.858 --> 00:38:30.650
In that case, you need
this line right here

00:38:30.650 --> 00:38:32.300
that says always sample.

00:38:32.300 --> 00:38:33.530
Always send that data.

00:38:33.530 --> 00:38:36.783
Don't do any 1%
magic or anything.

00:38:36.783 --> 00:38:38.950
There are a bunch of options
that you can look into,

00:38:38.950 --> 00:38:42.190
but I want to call that one out.

00:38:42.190 --> 00:38:44.640
Finally, you can
see in our handler

00:38:44.640 --> 00:38:47.640
we have this hello
span that we created.

00:38:47.640 --> 00:38:51.990
Like I said before,
OpenCensus has HTTP plugins

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:54.150
that will give you
even more information

00:38:54.150 --> 00:38:57.850
and help you debug
your web applications.

00:38:57.850 --> 00:38:59.970
So I wouldn't do it this
way if you're writing it.

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:04.740
Look into that OCHTTP package.

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:07.390
And then you get to
the bugs that I added.

00:39:07.390 --> 00:39:10.950
So it's a switch that creates
a random number that's

00:39:10.950 --> 00:39:13.410
either zero, one, or two.

00:39:13.410 --> 00:39:17.340
If it's a zero, we're going
to call this slow function.

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:20.920
If it's a one, we
call Fibonacci.

00:39:20.920 --> 00:39:23.030
So we'll start by
looking at slow,

00:39:23.030 --> 00:39:25.320
and you can see we have
a span that we created--

00:39:25.320 --> 00:39:27.540
hello.slow right
here-- and then we

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:31.240
call time.sleep for
300 milliseconds.

00:39:31.240 --> 00:39:33.910
Probably not something you
want to do in your production

00:39:33.910 --> 00:39:37.840
application, but it's
immediately obvious

00:39:37.840 --> 00:39:40.570
where to look and
how to fix this.

00:39:40.570 --> 00:39:43.810
Maybe, I don't know, we want
to sleep for 100 milliseconds,

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:44.890
or just none.

00:39:47.410 --> 00:39:48.790
And then we have Fibonacci.

00:39:48.790 --> 00:39:51.370
And Fibonacci returns
the Fibonacci number,

00:39:51.370 --> 00:39:53.030
and it does nothing special.

00:39:53.030 --> 00:39:55.690
It's just recursive,
has no caching,

00:39:55.690 --> 00:39:57.490
and it's immediately obvious--

00:39:57.490 --> 00:39:59.830
yes, this is something
that we could focus on.

00:39:59.830 --> 00:40:02.920
We can spend our developer
time and engineering effort

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:04.090
optimizing this function.

00:40:07.820 --> 00:40:13.610
And yeah.

00:40:13.610 --> 00:40:18.040
So with that, we started
with this Cloud Function.

00:40:18.040 --> 00:40:22.780
We added in a main.go,
a package main

00:40:22.780 --> 00:40:26.050
to call that function,
deployed that on App Engine,

00:40:26.050 --> 00:40:29.140
added a Docker file to
create our container image,

00:40:29.140 --> 00:40:31.630
and then deployed
that on Cloud Run.

00:40:31.630 --> 00:40:35.400
Then I showed you a few tips of
adding tracing and profiling.

00:40:35.400 --> 00:40:40.360
And so tracing is a little bit
higher effort than profiling.

00:40:40.360 --> 00:40:43.750
Profiling-- you have a few lines
of code that say, enable this.

00:40:43.750 --> 00:40:46.690
Tracing-- you have
to plumb context

00:40:46.690 --> 00:40:48.220
throughout your
entire application.

00:40:48.220 --> 00:40:50.833
And how many people have done
that, or at least tried to,

00:40:50.833 --> 00:40:52.750
where you look at context
and you're like, OK,

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:56.920
I need to pass this through
my entire application how?

00:40:56.920 --> 00:40:58.990
And I'm going to update how?

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:01.700
So that's kind of
a slow process.

00:41:01.700 --> 00:41:03.430
And, if you have a
very big application

00:41:03.430 --> 00:41:06.460
with a large team of
engineers working on it,

00:41:06.460 --> 00:41:10.270
one team might not have that
as their priority for a quarter

00:41:10.270 --> 00:41:12.650
when another does.

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:14.950
So it's helpful to
have those kind of two

00:41:14.950 --> 00:41:19.200
parts of the same
side, or the same coin,

00:41:19.200 --> 00:41:21.420
to get different visibility.

00:41:21.420 --> 00:41:24.120
Testing-- like I said,
tests should just be tests.

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:28.470
Your Go code should just
be Go code, hopefully

00:41:28.470 --> 00:41:30.930
with the caveat of you
have some authentication

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:33.390
requirements, depending
on where you're deploying

00:41:33.390 --> 00:41:35.800
and things like that.

00:41:35.800 --> 00:41:39.840
But the code that you're writing
should always look like Go code

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:42.270
without extra
surprises, just based

00:41:42.270 --> 00:41:45.170
on where you're deploying it.

00:41:45.170 --> 00:41:49.340
And, with that said, that's
all I have for today.

00:41:49.340 --> 00:41:51.230
Your feedback would be
greatly appreciated.

00:41:51.230 --> 00:41:53.823
You can do that in the app.

00:41:53.823 --> 00:41:55.240
And if you have
any questions, you

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:58.970
can either find me on Twitter,
or I'll be out in the hall

00:41:58.970 --> 00:42:01.020
after the talk.

00:42:01.020 --> 00:42:01.520
Thank you.

00:42:01.520 --> 00:42:04.870
